- Primary evidence: `Task_Authoring/task_inputs/plateform_report/6adfee3bd201142a513cf910_0_injection-1.md` (the rev1 FAIL; preserve unchanged; the rev2 PASS report now has the `…_0_injection.md` name). SQL revisions: `Working_Files/run_forensics/REVISIONS.md` (rev1 failed and is kept; rev2 passed /injection and is not stored; rev3 is current). Red-team rounds: `Working_Files/01b_Redteam_Review.md` and `.second.md`. Remediation record: `01_Scenario_Design.md` §13, rows P-F1 to P-A8.
- Regression audit of every finding: `Task_Authoring/task_inputs/Working_Files/plan/audit_platform.py` (32 checks over both platform reports; run it after every rebuild).

## 10. Search the whole universe for contradictions, not the persona's view

- Persona visibility decides findability. Contradiction checks must cover every mailbox, every Slack channel, Sheets, Drive and PDF attachments.
- All eight failures came from records outside Robert's mailbox: dbt and Metabase receipts in Leonard's mailbox, Zapier's plan email to Leonard, Leonard's Layer note in #prototype, iPostal1's closure notice, Unity's invoice in Arthur's mailbox, and the email introducing Sunset's CTO.
- Before seeding any financial or lifecycle fact about a vendor (unpaid or failed invoice, cancellation, end date, plan change, payment method), run a census of that vendor across all base services. Classify each hit as billing or other, and record locators in the dossier.
- Prefer vendors with no base billing footprint for injected financial states. Where the base has receipts, align with them; never override them.

## 11. A cost sheet is not payment status

- dbt's $143.85 and Metabase's $148 came from a base cost sheet ("HG Costs"). Receipts showed both invoices paid ($100 on 1 Feb, $148 on 21 Jan), so "declined" and "failed" contradicted them.
- Take payment status, amount and billing day from receipts, invoices and payment confirmations, never from budget or cost lines.

## 12. One wrong global assumption becomes hundreds of findings

- "Billed on the company Amex card" appeared in 119 issue descriptions and 119 comments. The base company card was the SVB MasterCard, Gusto was paid by bank transfer, and Meta Ads was on Leonard's own card.
- Verify any fact that repeats across many rows in the base before generating rows from it: card issuer, bank, payroll method, invoice schemes, tool names. Make per-account exceptions explicit.
- When a fact changes, change every row that restates it. Meta Ads' description was corrected but its statement-line comment still said "SVB card statement"; the red team caught the contradiction.

## 13. Read the full base record, then time and word the seeded fact after it

- Zapier's "Your Zapier plan has been updated" (12 Feb 21:55:54Z) says Professional is cancelled but usable until 27 Feb, then Free. It was read as "moved to free on 12 Feb", and the seeded comment was posted four hours before the email.
- Leonard's #prototype note said Layer access runs "until March 3rd"; the seed said "account closed".
- A seeded comment that reports an event the base records must come after that event and keep its meaning (end dates, "until" dates, what exactly was cancelled).
- If an email body is thin, read the attachment. Unity's invoice number, date, seats and tax were only in the PDF.

## 14. Confirm a person's role before reusing their token

- PERSON_3131 was used as Sunset's customer success manager because the token was cc'd on base Sunset mail. The base introduces PERSON_3131 as Sunset's CTO.
- Before reusing a person token, read the message that introduces them. If the base names a role only generically ("your dedicated customer success manager"), mint an unused token and prove its absence in every service.
- Base bodies tokenize names separately from headers: `[PERSON_NAME_…]` numbers do not map to `PERSON_NNNN` header numbers. Do not invent body tokens; check any you use.

## 15. Identifiers must follow the base series for the same vendor and product

- Unity Pro cited CINV_00025873, but CINV is the Unity Ads (uAds) invoice series from a different sender. The Pro seats bill as INZ… invoices.
- PlaytestCloud's base invoices are 87931-YYYY-N, charged to the card per order. The seeded "PTC-2041" emailed invoice used an invented scheme (advisory).
- Seeded identifiers follow the vendor's base scheme and sender. When citing a base number, confirm its product, sender and date.

## 16. Check an account's lifecycle before giving it a trap

- iPostal1 carried the "Leonard takes it onto his legal tab" trap, but the base shows its mailbox closed on 16 Jan; the seed even gave it a February plan.
- Before assigning a trap to an account, check closures, cancellations, plan changes and keep/cancel lists in the base. Put the trap on an account whose base record is silent or consistent.
- Swapping trap roles among existing accounts kept every Linear key unchanged, because keys follow the sorted account names. Prefer that to adding or renaming accounts late.

## 17. Chronology means "after the event the text relies on"

- Batch-timed notes (every moved account at 11 Feb 22:30Z) said "Arthur is carrying it" for GitHub, dbt and Metabase, but Arthur announced the KubLLC move on 12 Feb 23:00Z. The platform did not flag it; a stricter review would.
- For each seeded text, list the base events it names (decisions, cancellations, signings, card moves) and check that the text is later than the event's first base record. Do not let a shared timestamp template override per-account history.
- Validate every cited base pointer by lookup. The account plan cited Slack `C04UEQVDVB7/1770835954.458779`, which does not exist.

## 18. Appending to a base thread changes derived thread fields

- The seeded Sherwood reply became the last message of three base threads, but `gmail_threads.snippet` still showed the old last message (advisory A8).
- Add an UPDATE for every derived column an append changes. For Gmail threads that is only `snippet` (live columns: id, user_email, snippet, history_id).

## 19. Remediation mechanics that worked

- Fix the generators, never the SQL by hand. Rebuild in dependency order (`plan/rebuild.sh --final`), then rerun gates in chain order: injection → error-async → prompt-writing → prompt-check. Freshness rules require each output to be newer than its inputs, so even an unchanged decision (error-async) needs a recorded re-review and a re-paste.
- A truth change ripples into hand-written lists: the brief's accepted outcomes (`assemble_brief.py`), the check report (`gen_check.py`), dossier sections and the scenario template. After regenerating, grep every artifact for the old total, counts, vendor names, invoice ids and tokens.
- Ask the red team to act as the platform reviewer: for every vendor, person and global fact the SQL names, search the whole base for contradicting records, and check text-versus-event chronology. Self-consistency review alone missed every finding.
- Keep the failed revision for provenance and store the fix as the next revision.
- Research subagents repeatedly ran out of step budget. Give each a narrow, itemized brief with ready helper scripts, and require incremental writes of findings.

